(contributing to the conversation by defending an alternative point of vi\ew.)Tj0.01711 Tc -0.0139 Tw 1 -1.2778 Td(In this segment Tyisha both articulates and enacts a position of opposi-\)Tj0.00571 Tc -0.0058 Tw -1 -1.2778 Td(tion or resistance. She argues that someone marginalized, like Kino, sho\uld)Tj0.01711 Tc 0.25999 Tw T*(oppose the powerful and stand up for himself. She also defends her)Tj0.1666 Tw T*(opinion against several others in the class. She habitually opposes the)Tj0.01669 Tw T*(teachers and other students by defending unpopular positions. So in this\)Tj0.0186 Tw T*(discussion Tyisha enacts what she recommends that marginalized people)Tj0.13741 Tw T*(should be doing. People like Kino who stick up for themselves are not)Tj0.1739 Tw T*(greedy, but are justi\336ably defending their interests against others w\ho )Tj0.06689 Tw T*(take advantage of them. Mrs Bailey\325s favourable response to Tyisha he\re)Tj0.00121 Tc -0.0013 Tw T*(endorses Tyisha\325s position. Sticking up for oneself with reasoned arg\uments)Tj0.00909 Tc -0.0092 Tw T*(in classroom discussion is valued by the teachers, just as Kino\325s dec\ision to)Tj0.0056 Tc -0.00571 Tw T*(stick up for himself is valued by Tyisha.)Tj0.01711 Tc 0.15669 Tw 1 -1.2778 Td(In this class on 12 April, then, Tyisha gets explicitly labelled as and)Tj0.0043 Tc -0.00439 Tw -1 -1.2778 Td(enacts both emergent identities \320 as a disruptive outcast and as a le\gitimate)Tj0.006 Tc -0.0061 Tw T*(dissenter. The \336rst identity emerged and solidi\336ed from December t\hrough)Tj0.015 Tc -0.01511 Tw T*(February, with respect to the individualism/collectivism curricular them\e,)Tj0.01711 Tc -0.00079 Tw T*(and it remains available as teachers and students identify her throughou\t)Tj0.0141 Tw T*(the rest of the school year. The second identity became available in Apr\il)Tj0.0034 Tw T*(and thereafter. At the end of the school year, Tyisha was sometimes posi\-)Tj0.0276